<!-- ===================== SECTION 1 ===================== -->
<h2>1. Domain Area of the Project</h2>

<table>
  <tr>
    <th>Domain Type</th>
    <th>Area</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><b>Primary Domain</b></td>
    <td>Web Development (Full-Stack Java)</td>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td><b>Secondary Domain</b></td>
    <td>Web Development + Cloud Computing + DevOps (Docker, Prometheus, Grafana)</td>
  </tr>
</table>

<p><b>Justification:</b> RideX is primarily a full-stack web application built using Spring Boot 3.4 (Java 17+) with Thymeleaf server-side rendering and a MySQL 8.0 database. The secondary domain extends into Cloud Computing and DevOps through containerized deployment using Docker Compose, cloud-based image storage via Cloudinary, and production-grade system monitoring with Prometheus and Grafana. The application also integrates external APIs such as OSRM (Open Source Routing Machine) for real-time route computation and OpenStreetMap/Leaflet.js for interactive map visualization, blending geospatial computing with traditional web development.</p>

<hr class="section-divider">

<!-- ===================== SECTION 2 ===================== -->
<h2>2. Summary of Project (Problem Statement, Literature, Proposed Solution)</h2>

<h3>2.1 Problem Statement</h3>

<p>Urban commuting in India is increasingly unsustainable. According to the TomTom Traffic Index 2024, Indian cities like Bengaluru, Mumbai, and Delhi rank among the top 10 most congested cities globally, with commuters spending an average of 30–45 extra minutes per trip due to traffic congestion. The root cause is clear: over 85% of personal vehicles on Indian roads carry only a single occupant, leading to massive road space wastage, fuel over-consumption, and environmental degradation. The transportation sector contributes approximately 13.5% of India's total CO₂ emissions (IEA, 2023), and single-occupancy vehicles are the primary contributor within this segment.</p>

<p>Despite the evident need, existing informal carpooling methods — WhatsApp groups, office bulletin boards, or word-of-mouth arrangements — suffer from critical shortcomings: <b>lack of trust</b> (no identity verification), <b>absence of route optimization</b> (no map-based matching), <b>no accountability</b> (no rating or review systems), and <b>zero incentivization</b> (no reward for repeated sharing). These barriers prevent widespread adoption of carpooling as a viable daily commute alternative.</p>

<h3>2.2 Industry / Society Benefitted</h3>

<p>RideX directly benefits the following groups:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Daily Commuters &amp; Students:</b> Expected to reduce individual commute costs by up to 60–75% through ride sharing, with an average saving of ₹2,000–₹4,000 per month for a typical 20 km daily commute.</li>
  <li><b>Urban Traffic Systems:</b> By consolidating 3–4 single-occupancy trips into one shared ride, the platform is expected to reduce the number of vehicles on road by approximately 25–30% among active user groups, alleviating traffic congestion.</li>
  <li><b>Environment:</b> Each shared ride eliminates an estimated 2.4 kg of CO₂ per trip (assuming 15 km average distance). For a user base of 1,000 active riders completing 3 rides per week, this translates to approximately 374 tonnes of CO₂ reduction annually.</li>
  <li><b>Educational Institutions &amp; IT Parks:</b> Concentrated commuter clusters (colleges, tech parks) benefit the most due to similar source-destination patterns, making ride matching highly efficient.</li>
</ul>

<h3>2.3 Presently Available Solutions / Literature</h3>

<table>
  <tr>
    <th>#</th>
    <th>Solution / Research Work</th>
    <th>Type</th>
    <th>Key Feature</th>
  </tr>
  <tr>
    <td>1</td>
    <td><b>BlaBlaCar</b> (blablacar.in)</td>
